"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_tma_site=self.webpackChunk_tma_site||[]).push([[2878],{5595(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/10/15/iteration-2b","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/15/iteration-2b","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-15-iteration-2b.md","title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","description":"Iteration 2b asks one question: does weighting speed in the loss get the iteration-2 models to the","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":7.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2"}},"content":"Iteration 2b asks one question: does weighting speed in the loss get the iteration-2 models to the\\nG2 speed threshold? It changes nothing else, and judges G2 on a fresh test split.\\n\\n**Verdict: G2 failed** (agent review panel, round 2, no rejections).\\n\\n- **Range passes** for both models: 14.6% [14.1, 15.2] instantaneous and 15.8% [15.2, 16.2]\\n  time-late at zig + 300 s on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, against 20%.\\n- **Course passes for both:** 8.22\xb0 [7.88, 8.54] and 8.66\xb0 [8.29, 9.03], against 10\xb0. The\\n  time-late course failure of iteration 2 is gone.\\n- **Speed fails for both, by less:** 1.77 kt [1.68, 1.83] and 1.77 kt [1.67, 1.86] at\\n  zig + 600 s, against 1 kt. The same weights before 2b, scored on the same scenarios, were at\\n  2.02 kt and 2.31 kt. Classical reaches 0.60 kt.\\n\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261015-i2c-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261015-i2c-g2).\\nCurves and slices: [iteration-2b results](/docs/results/iteration-2b).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\nThe SRD allows one more round after a stalled G2. Iteration 2 left three leads (research R19):\\nderived features, speed in the loss, and the training budget. The user chose the loss (decisions\\n15\u201317 in spec 004):\\n\\n- **Loss weight.** The speed term of the loss is weighted 4\xd7 (`loss_weight_speed: 4`). Speed\\n  contributed about 15% of the loss before; now it is about 40%.\\n- **Continued, not restarted.** Every model (the two carried ones and the three controls) starts\\n  from its committed phase-B weights, to 220 epochs at most with patience 6. Because the loss\\n  changed, `tma-train` re-scores the best epoch under the new loss before it resumes; otherwise\\n  the old best, on the old scale, could never be beaten and the old weights would be kept.\\n- **A fresh test split.** The iteration-2 test split (seed 201) decided the first attempt and is\\n  never scored again. The gate is judged on the test split of seed 202, generated with the same\\n  count and ranges and pinned by content digest in the training plan. Train and validation are\\n  unchanged.\\n\\nThree tooling checks promised at the end of iteration 2 arrived with 2b and were on for this\\ngate: `tma-g2` checks the plan\'s content digests itself, refuses a phase decision that is not a\\ngit ancestor of the carried weights, and checks that every estimator was scored on the same rows,\\nnot just the same number of them. The [evaluation](/docs/evaluation) page describes them.\\n\\nThe carried set was fixed before training: the 2b models, unconditionally. Phase B is scored\\nbeside them on the fresh split and reported, never gated.\\n\\n## Results on the fresh test split (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                        | Range, zig+300 s       | Course, zig+300 s      | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                     | < 20%                  | < 10\xb0                  | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (2b)          | **14.6%** [14.1, 15.2] | **8.22\xb0** [7.88, 8.54] | 1.77 kt [1.68, 1.83] |\\n| bilstm-time-late (2b)            | **15.8%** [15.2, 16.2] | **8.66\xb0** [8.29, 9.03] | 1.77 kt [1.67, 1.86] |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (B, reported) | 16.5%                  | 9.20\xb0                  | 2.02 kt              |\\n| bilstm-time-late (B, reported)   | 19.2%                  | 10.36\xb0                 | 2.31 kt              |\\n| classical                        | 8.5%                   | 3.31\xb0                  | 0.60 kt              |\\n| naive                            | 37.9%                  | 90.4\xb0                  | 4.22 kt              |\\n\\nn = 4,682 scenarios at zig + 300 s (93% of the slice) and 2,973 at zig + 600 s (59%).\\n\\nPhase B\'s numbers here differ from the iteration-2 verdict (2.09 / 2.38 kt) because this is a\\ndifferent split; the intervals overlap.\\n\\n### The repeated G1 checks\\n\\n| Check                                  | Result                                   |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\\n| instantaneous / naive < 0.5            | pass: 0.418 [0.405, 0.432]               |\\n| time-late / naive < 0.5                | pass: 0.430 [0.419, 0.443]               |\\n| time-late not worse than instantaneous | fail: 2 of 19 points (t = 840 and 960 s) |\\n| both models beat their controls        | pass                                     |\\n| shuffled-label model at naive          | pass: 1.000 \xd7                            |\\n\\nThe time-late model\'s knife-edge G1 fail from iteration 2 (0.514) is now a clear pass. Its\\ndeficit since G1 was a training deficit, not an architectural one: given the same budget it\\nmatches the instantaneous model on every check. The \\"not worse\\" check has no interval; the two\\nexceedances (ratios 1.055 and 1.056 against 1.05) have paired intervals that contain 1.05, so\\nthis fail is not distinguishable from a pass. What is real is the G1 caveat persisting: the\\ntime-late model is slightly worse than the instantaneous one at 17 of 19 points, not better.\\n\\n## What the speed weight did, and did not do\\n\\n- **Speed moved by 12\u201323%** (2.02 \u2192 1.77 kt; 2.31 \u2192 1.77 kt), and range and course improved with\\n  it. Nothing got worse.\\n- **It is still 1.8 kt against 1 kt**, with the whole interval above 1.67 kt. The models are\\n  about three times classical, which is at the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound on these bearings: the panel\\n  recomputed the per-scenario bound on this split (median 1.05 kt for the bearings to\\n  zig + 600 s) and classical\'s error is 0.62 of it at the median, where an efficient estimator\\n  gives 0.67.\\n- **The gain is less scatter as much as less shrinkage.** The slope of predicted on true speed\\n  rose from 0.58 to 0.65 and the residual spread fell from 2.38 to 2.21 kt; correcting the\\n  shrinkage alone would not help (it would give 2.3 kt). Target speeds above ownship\'s 15 kt map\\n  to normalised targets above 1, where the shrinkage is worst; the output scaling was not changed.\\n- **The gap is where classical is near-perfect.** By true range at zig + 600 s (all noise):\\n\\n| True range         | Instantaneous | Time-late | Classical |\\n| ------------------ | ------------- | --------- | --------- |\\n| 2\u20135 km (n 731)     | 1.44 kt       | 1.43 kt   | 0.17 kt   |\\n| 5\u201310 km (n 2,219)  | 1.55 kt       | 1.53 kt   | 0.57 kt   |\\n| 10\u201320 km (n 2,568) | 2.12 kt       | 2.01 kt   | 1.51 kt   |\\n| 20\u201330 km (n 420)   | 2.98 kt       | 3.08 kt   | 3.54 kt   |\\n\\nClassical\'s 0.60 kt median is carried by the short ranges, where a few hundred metres of range\\nerror fixes speed to a fraction of a knot. The models are 1.4\u20131.5 kt there, flat across bands\\nthey resolve well, and only beyond 20 km do they beat classical on speed.\\n\\n- **The range advantage beyond 10 km is narrower than iteration 2 suggested.** At zig + 300 s\\n  the models are ahead at 10\u201320 km (14.2% against 18.6%, all noise) and at 20\u201330 km (24.1%\\n  against 27.4%), but the gap is carried by classical\'s non-converged tail (117 of 1,806 scenarios\\n  at 10\u201320 km on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, scored as infinite error). On converged scenarios alone the two\\n  are level, and at zig + 600 s classical is better in every band.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers again: TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics,\\nreproducibility, and red team. All five returned approve-with-findings in round 1, and none\\nrejected. Every one of them reproduced the speed medians independently, and none found a path\\nfrom a pipeline error to the verdict.\\n\\n**One latent major, fixed.** When a continuation changes the loss, `tma-train` re-scores the best\\nweights under the new loss before it resumes. It used that value in memory but never wrote it to\\nthe manifest, so a chained job (resuming with its optimizer state) compared new-loss epochs against\\nthe old-loss number. Had a continuation\'s first job ended before any epoch beat the re-scored\\nvalue, later jobs would have kept the old weights silently. It did not happen in this run (the\\nreviewer checked every job boundary in the CI logs), but it could have. The manifest now records\\nthe value a later epoch must beat and the source of a continuation, a chained-job test covers the\\ncase, and `tma-train --check` verifies that a finished continuation\'s kept weights are explained\\nby its record.\\n\\n**What else the panel established:**\\n\\n- The fresh split is fresh: per-scenario seeds derive from the dataset seed and index, a seed-201\\n  and seed-202 collision would need an index difference of 10\xb9\u2078, and no test scenario shares a\\n  seed or parameter set with any of the 100,000 seed-201 scenarios.\\n- The fail margin is 16\u201320 standard errors. Only 30% of scenarios have a speed error under 1 kt;\\n  for the interval merely to touch the threshold the test set would have to be about 28 scenarios.\\n- The re-scoring worked as specified: the reviewers recomputed the re-scored losses to five\\n  decimals and confirmed each model\'s best epoch was chosen under the new loss. The shuffled\\n  control\'s weights are unchanged from phase B because no epoch beat its re-scored loss, which is\\n  the right outcome for a control.\\n- Classical\'s non-converged scenarios (1.4%) are scored as infinite error and included; its\\n  0.60 kt is not flattered.\\n\\nNineteen minor findings were answered or fixed in two rounds, mostly record-keeping: the\\nmanifests of the committed 2b models do not say they are continuations (the facts are in the\\nreview), gate-deciding seeds are now reserved in the plan checker, and the write-up wording above\\nfollows the geometry reviewer\'s corrections.\\n\\nRecord:\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261015-i2c-g2/review.md).\\n\\n## Next\\n\\nSRD \xa77 was amended on 2026-10-15 to allow one further round, iteration 2c, and then stop. The\\ncapacity scan (research R20) showed that a two-layer 128-unit LSTM reaches the 96-unit model\'s\\nplateau in half the epochs. Iteration 2c trains that model from scratch with the speed weight, on\\nyet another fresh test split (seed 203). The decision record, with the forecast (1.4\u20131.6 kt) and\\nthe stopping rule, is committed before training:\\n[`experiments/20261015-i2d-decision/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261015-i2d-decision)."},{"id":"/2026/10/14/iteration-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-14-iteration-2.md","title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","description":"Iteration 2 asks whether the iteration-1 models give a useful estimate on realistic data: noisy","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":7.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/15/iteration-2b"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1"}},"content":"Iteration 2 asks whether the iteration-1 models give a _useful_ estimate on realistic data: noisy\\nbearings, dropouts and a rate-limited ownship turn.\\n\\n**Verdict: G2 failed** (agent review panel, round 2, no rejections).\\n\\n- **Range passes** for both models. Median range error at zig + 300 s on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice is\\n  16.5% [16.1, 17.1] instantaneous and 18.5% [17.7, 19.1] time-late, against a 20% threshold.\\n- **Course passes for the instantaneous model only:** 9.46\xb0 [9.02, 9.90]. The time-late model\\n  fails at 10.57\xb0 [10.16, 11.04], against 10\xb0.\\n- **Speed fails decisively for both:** 2.09 kt [2.00, 2.18] and 2.38 kt [2.28, 2.48] at\\n  zig + 600 s, against 1 kt. Classical reaches 0.62 kt on the same scenarios.\\n\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261014-i2-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261014-i2-g2).\\nCurves and slices: [iteration-2 results](/docs/results/iteration-2).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed from iteration 1\\n\\n- **Noise and dropouts.**\\n  - Bearing noise \u03c3 \u2208 {1\xb0, 2\xb0}, half the scenarios each.\\n  - Dropouts drawn per sample, at 0\u201310% per scenario.\\n  - G2 is judged on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice. The \u03c3 = 2\xb0 slice and the two dropout bands are reported.\\n- **A rate-limited turn.**\\n  - Ownship\'s zig is now flown at 0.5\u20133\xb0/s instead of instantaneously.\\n  - The zig time is the end of the turn, so \\"zig + 300 s\\" means five minutes after ownship is\\n    steady on its new leg.\\n  - This changed the generator (0.2.0), so G0 was re-run and passed again (panel rounds 1\u20133,\\n    [`20261013-g0-i2-r2`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261013-g0-i2-r2)).\\n- **Provenance.**\\n  - Every dataset header names the generator\'s commit.\\n  - Every model records the header of the data it was trained on.\\n  - `tma-train` refuses to resume on different features. `tma-g2` refuses to score the test\\n    split before the phase decision is committed.\\n- **Confidence intervals.**\\n  - Every gated median carries a seeded bootstrap 95% interval.\\n  - A check whose interval contains its threshold is [inconclusive](/docs/glossary), not a pass\\n    or fail.\\n\\n## Two training phases, one test score\\n\\nThe test split was protected by a two-phase rule, fixed before training (research R5).\\n\\n**Phase A** ran the G1 configurations unchanged: 60 epochs, patience 3, one seed per model. On\\nvalidation, the instantaneous model passed range (17.4%) and failed course (10.47\xb0) and speed\\n(2.24 kt), so phase A stalled. The decision record was committed before phase B started:\\n[`decision.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261013-i2a-val/decision.md).\\n\\n**Phase B** made one change, the training budget. Every model continued from its phase-A weights\\nto 150 epochs with patience 6. This was the user\'s choice over the pre-registered default\\n(derived features), and the record says what it tests: whether undertraining explains the gap.\\n\\nPhase B improved range (16.6%) and course (9.56\xb0) on validation, but not speed (2.12 kt). It was\\ncarried by the pre-declared rule: its range error was no worse than phase A\'s by more than phase\\nA\'s interval half-width.\\n\\nThe test split was then scored **once**, for phase B, with phase A beside it.\\n\\n## Results on test (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                        | Range, zig+300 s       | Course, zig+300 s      | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                     | < 20%                  | < 10\xb0                  | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (B)           | **16.5%** [16.1, 17.1] | **9.46\xb0** [9.02, 9.90] | 2.09 kt [2.00, 2.18] |\\n| bilstm-time-late (B)             | **18.5%** [17.7, 19.1] | 10.57\xb0 [10.16, 11.04]  | 2.38 kt [2.28, 2.48] |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (A, reported) | 17.7%                  | 10.45\xb0                 | 2.26 kt              |\\n| bilstm-time-late (A, reported)   | 20.0%                  | 11.24\xb0                 | 2.64 kt              |\\n| classical                        | 8.8%                   | 3.42\xb0                  | 0.62 kt              |\\n| naive                            | 37.0%                  | 89.9\xb0                  | 4.25 kt              |\\n\\nn = 4,707 scenarios at zig + 300 s (93% of the slice) and 2,995 at zig + 600 s (59%).\\n\\n### The repeated G1 checks\\n\\nThese are judged on all rows.\\n\\n| Check                                  | Result                                      |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\\n| instantaneous / naive < 0.5            | pass: 0.472 [0.458, 0.484]                  |\\n| time-late / naive < 0.5                | fail: 0.514 [0.500, 0.529], on the boundary |\\n| time-late not worse than instantaneous | fail, as at G1                              |\\n| both models beat their controls        | pass                                        |\\n| shuffled-label model at naive          | pass: 1.015 \xd7                               |\\n\\nThe time-late fail sits on a knife-edge. The interval\'s lower bound is 0.50035, and with other\\nbootstrap seeds it falls between 0.4995 and 0.5006. It does not decide the gate.\\n\\n## Why speed fails\\n\\nThe panel ruled out the pipeline:\\n\\n- Three reviewers re-predicted phase B independently and got exactly 2.09 kt and 2.38 kt.\\n- The model\'s mean speed is right: 11.58 kt predicted against 11.69 kt true.\\n\\nThe errors come from shrinkage towards the mean instead:\\n\\n- The slope of predicted against true speed is about 0.57.\\n- Slow targets are over-estimated and fast ones under-estimated: errors are 2.6 kt at 0\u20135 kt and\\n  2.9 kt at 15\u201320 kt, against 1.5 kt at 10\u201315 kt.\\n\\nThe information is there. A per-scenario Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound on the actual bearings gives a median\\nachievable speed error of 0.64 kt, and classical reaches 0.62 kt. The models are about 3.3 times\\nthe bound.\\n\\nThree things point to how the models are trained, not to a ceiling of the approach:\\n\\n- **Speed gets little weight in the loss.** All four loss terms are weighted equally, and speed\\n  contributes about 15% of the loss.\\n- **Training stopped early.** In phase B the training loss was still falling (LSTM 0.400 \u2192 0.377\\n  over its last 14 epochs). Validation loss moved by about \xb13% from epoch to epoch, more than the\\n  per-epoch gain, so patience 6 ended the run.\\n- **More epochs barely moved speed.** The phase-B change improved range and course but not\\n  speed.\\n\\n## Where the models do well\\n\\nRange bands at zig + 300 s, all noise levels:\\n\\n| True range         | Instantaneous         | Classical          | Naive |\\n| ------------------ | --------------------- | ------------------ | ----- |\\n| 2\u20135 km (n 1,438)   | 20.0%                 | 5.3%               | 147%  |\\n| 5\u201310 km (n 3,486)  | 15.3%                 | 9.5%               | 23.0% |\\n| 10\u201320 km (n 3,728) | **16.7%**, gross 2.3% | 18.5%, gross 24.7% | 31.9% |\\n| 20\u201330 km (n 534)   | 31.4%                 | 28.7%              | 58.6% |\\n\\nShort ranges carry classical\'s advantage. Beyond 10 km the models match or beat it on range, and\\nfail grossly far less often. The same holds at \u03c3 = 2\xb0: the instantaneous model\'s range error\\nmoves only from 16.5% to 18.4%, while classical\'s doubles from 8.8% to 17.1%.\\n\\nOn held-out geometry, with initial range 20\u201330 km (outside training, reported only), the models\\nbeat classical on every median:\\n\\n| Estimator | Range      | Course    | Speed        |\\n| --------- | ---------- | --------- | ------------ |\\n| Models    | 43.8\u201344.8% | about 36\xb0 | about 3.6 kt |\\n| Classical | 48.8%      | 50.9\xb0     | 9.2 kt       |\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers: TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics, reproducibility,\\nand red team. All five returned approve-with-findings in round 1, and none rejected.\\n\\n**Two major findings, both resolved:**\\n\\n- **Phase A\'s predictions overwrote phase B\'s on disk.** `tma-g2` wrote both phases\' prediction\\n  files under the same names. The verdict was unaffected, because it was scored in memory, but\\n  re-scoring from disk would have given phase A\'s numbers. Fixed: the other phase\'s files are now\\n  tagged.\\n- **The models were stopped by noise, not converged.** Accepted. The verdict is stated for this\\n  architecture, these inputs and this budget.\\n\\n**A gate-rule fix.** The G2 code failed a check whose interval straddled its threshold whenever\\nthe point estimate was at or above it. The spec says that case is inconclusive. The code now\\nfollows the spec, and a check passes only if its point estimate is also below the threshold.\\n\\nRe-gating the committed metrics with the new code gave identical checks, and the reproducibility\\nreviewer re-ran the whole gate byte for byte.\\n\\nRound 2 approved with only minor findings open. Record:\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261014-i2-g2/review.md).\\n\\n## What G2 cannot tell us\\n\\n- **The speed point covers 59% of scenarios.** Only windows that run at least ten minutes past\\n  the turn are scored at zig + 600 s, so the figure describes longer windows (decision 13). At\\n  zig + 300 s, where 93% are scored, speed is still 2.30 kt.\\n- **Medians hide the range dependence.** The range pass is not carried by short ranges, which are\\n  the models\' weak band. But a single median mixes a 10\u201320 km band where the models beat\\n  classical with a 2\u20135 km band where they are four times worse.\\n- **One seed per model** (decision 11). The intervals cover test-set sampling, not\\n  training-to-training variation.\\n- **The course pass is narrow.** Its interval\'s upper bound is 9.90\xb0 against a 10\xb0 threshold.\\n- **Targets never manoeuvre.** G2 says nothing about tracking a target\'s own zig.\\n- **Not done:** scoring the G1 models on a turn-only set (reported only, not gated). It is\\n  deferred to iteration 2b.\\n\\n## Next: iteration 2b\\n\\nThe SRD allows one more round after a stalled G2 (decision 5). The leads, in research R6, R18 and\\nR19:\\n\\n1. **Derived features.** Bearing rate and the bearing-line intercept, already built\\n   (`feature_set: derived`), give the network quantities classical TMA works from.\\n2. **Speed in the loss.** Weight it per term, or scale its target to unit variance.\\n3. **Per-leg steady-track fit.** Within each ownship leg, fit tan B(t) = (x\u2080 + v\u2093t)/(y\u2080 + v_yt)\\n   to the bearings. The fitted shape constrains relative velocity without needing range, and the\\n   residual flags a target manoeuvre.\\n4. **Budget and stopping.** A smoothed stopping criterion or a longer run, since training loss\\n   was still falling.\\n\\nBefore 2b scores a gate, three tooling fixes are due:\\n\\n- `tma-g2` checks the plan\'s content digests itself;\\n- the decision commit must be an ancestor of the weights\' commit;\\n- the same-rows check compares row identity, not just counts."},{"id":"/2026/10/12/iteration-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-12-iteration-1.md","title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","description":"Iteration 1 asks the question the whole project exists for: given simulated bearings and the","date":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":24.98,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","date":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified"}},"content":"Iteration 1 asks the question the whole project exists for: given simulated bearings and the\\nownship track, can a small neural network estimate target range better than a guess from the\\nprior?\\n\\n**Verdict: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat.** Both model variants beat the naive baseline\\nby more than 2\xd7 at the gate point, with the whole 95% interval below 0.5: instantaneous 0.419\\n[0.407, 0.431], time-late 0.450 [0.438, 0.464] (medians 16.1% and 17.3%; naive 38.4%, classical\\n4.6%). The shuffled-label model scores at naive. The machine verdict is **inconclusive**, because\\nthe time-late model is measurably _worse_ than the instantaneous one (1.075 [1.046, 1.108] \xd7 at\\nzig + 300 s) although it sees more data; the agent review panel passed G1 under a rule fixed\\nbefore the deciding run (pre-registered rule 1, SRD \xa75) and recorded that as the caveat.\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1-r2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261012-g1-r2).\\n\\nIt took two attempts. In **attempt 1** (`experiments/20261011-g1-r2/`) the time-late model was cut\\noff by a wall-clock cap after 25 of its 60 epochs and failed (0.551 [0.538, 0.565]). One\\npre-registered continuation of that model, to early stopping at 50 epochs, gave the pass. Full\\ncurves and slices: [iteration-1 results](/docs/results/iteration-1).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The question\\n\\nG1 ([SRD \xa75](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/docs/SRD.md#5-baselines-metrics-and-success-gates))\\npasses if, for **both** model variants, the median relative range error 5 minutes after the\\nownship zig is below half of the [naive baseline](/docs/algorithms/naive-baseline)\'s, on the same\\ntest scenarios. The naive baseline predicts the training-set mean range, course and speed, so a\\nmodel that beats it by 2\xd7 has learned something about the geometry, not just the prior.\\n\\nThe two variants answer different questions. The **instantaneous** model is causal: its estimate\\nat a given time uses only bearings up to that time, as a real-time tracker would. The\\n**time-late** model sees the whole recorded window, including bearings after the gate point, and\\nso is a post-hoc reconstruction.\\n\\nEach ratio is judged with a paired bootstrap 95% interval: pass if the whole interval is below 0.5,\\nfail if it is wholly at or above, otherwise inconclusive. Further checks guard the result: a model\\ntrained on shuffled labels must score like naive (leakage), each recurrent model must beat a\\nnon-recurrent control, and time-late must not be worse than instantaneous. How these combine is on\\nthe [evaluation](/docs/evaluation#g1--checks-and-how-they-combine) page.\\n\\n## What was trained\\n\\nFour small models over the same masked 240-row input window (see [models](/docs/algorithms/models)),\\nplus one leakage run, trained on GitHub-hosted runners (4 cores, no GPU). Every model had\\n`max_epochs` 60, patience 3 on validation loss and a 5.5 h wall-clock cap (`max_wall_s` 19,800;\\n2 h for the shuffled run). Epochs are counted from 0, so \\"60 (best 58)\\" means the 59th epoch gave\\nthe kept weights.\\n\\n| Model                         | Role                             | Layers                                | Epochs (best) | Stop reason    | Wall time  | s / epoch | Runner CPU                |\\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------- | ---------- | --------- | ------------------------- |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`          | instantaneous variant (causal)   | LSTM 96                               | 60 (58)       | `max_epochs`   | 4 h 36 min | 276       | AMD EPYC 9V45             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`            | time-late variant (whole window) | BiLSTM 96                             | 25 (23)       | `wall_clock`   | 5 h 23 min | 775       | AMD EPYC 9V74             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`          | control for instantaneous        | 3 \xd7 causal Conv1D 64, k = 15          | 21 (17)       | early stopping | 53 min     | 152       | AMD EPYC 9V74             |\\n| `mlp-control`                 | control for time-late            | MLP 256-256 over the flattened window | 24 (20)       | early stopping | 7 min      | 17        | AMD EPYC 7763             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled` | leakage check                    | LSTM 96 on shuffled labels            | 8 (4)         | early stopping | 1 h 22 min | 617       | Intel Xeon Platinum 8370C |\\n\\nData: 100,000 scenarios (seed 101), \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0, no dropouts, initial range 2\u201320 km log-uniform;\\n80,000 train, 10,000 validation, 10,000 test, plus 10,000 held-out scenarios at 20\u201330 km.\\n\\nThe `bilstm-time-late` row is attempt 1. Under the protocol below it was continued once, from the\\nepoch-23 weights with no wall-clock cap, and stopped early after 50 epochs (best 46); the other\\nfour models are unchanged. One configuration per model; nothing was tuned. In attempt 1 the\\ntime-late configuration was started twice with identical settings (one job cancelled, one\\ncompleted).\\n\\n### Every run (FR-009)\\n\\n- [38131616871](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38131616871), `train/i1`, 11 Oct:\\n  all five models. Instantaneous, CNN, MLP and shuffled completed; these four are the models of\\n  record. The time-late job was cancelled after \u2248 5 h 15 min; the g1 job then stopped at once\\n  (`time-late: training has not finished`). Test split not scored.\\n- [38152208243](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38152208243), `train/i1`, 11 Oct:\\n  time-late only, attempt 1 (5 h 24 min job, AMD EPYC 9V74); the other four reused. Its g1 job\\n  scored the test split but could not push its results branch; informational only (same models\\n  as the attempt-1 re-score).\\n- [38153046011](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38153046011) and\\n  [38154023864](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38154023864), `train/ckpt-smoke`:\\n  checkpoint smoke tests (MLP); g1 stopped at model collection. Test split not scored.\\n- [38165442543](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38165442543), `train/i1`: cancelled\\n  25 s after it was queued; no job ran.\\n- [38178916962](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38178916962): re-score\\n  (`evaluate-g1.yml`) of the committed models, failed before any model was scored. The test\\n  features, regenerated at schema 0.5.0, carry a different header digest from the 0.4.0 training\\n  data, so prediction refused them (\\"normalisation differs\\"). Fixed by PR #27 (accepts a\\n  schema-version-only relabel).\\n- [38182021648](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38182021648): re-score with PR #27,\\n  **attempt 1 of record**, `experiments/20261011-g1-r2/`.\\n- [38208186337](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38208186337), `train/i1-continue`,\\n  12 Oct: the pre-registered time-late continuation, over two run attempts (jobs listed under\\n  attempt 2 below). Its final g1 job scored the test split into `experiments/20261012-g1/`, the\\n  training run\'s own folder (train/i1 code, no bootstrap; its `verdict.json` reads `fail`):\\n  **informational only**.\\n- [38275763335](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38275763335): re-score of all five\\n  committed models on main code (`evaluate-g1.yml`), **attempt 2 of record**,\\n  `experiments/20261012-g1-r2/`.\\n\\n## Attempt 1: results at the gate point (zig + 300 s, test split)\\n\\nExperiment `20261011-g1-r2`, re-scored from the committed weights by run 38182021648. n = 9,784\\nscenarios; 216 excluded because their window ends before the gate point; the same scenarios for\\nevery estimator.\\n\\n| Estimator                              | Range median | Range P90 | Course median | Course P90 | Speed median | Speed P90 | Gross-error rate |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------ | --------- | ------------- | ---------- | ------------ | --------- | ---------------- |\\n| naive                                  | 38.4%        | 161.5%    | 90.0\xb0         | 163.0\xb0     | 4.30 kt      | 7.67 kt   | 35.7%            |\\n| classical (per-timestep least squares) | 4.6%         | 29.2%     | 1.9\xb0          | 23.7\xb0      | 0.64 kt      | 5.19 kt   | 5.7%             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`                   | 16.1%        | 39.6%     | 9.4\xb0          | 41.7\xb0      | 2.16 kt      | 5.59 kt   | 5.4%             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`                     | 21.2%        | 49.6%     | 11.5\xb0         | 48.4\xb0      | 2.84 kt      | 6.28 kt   | 9.9%             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`                   | 29.3%        | 69.7%     | 23.0\xb0         | 78.4\xb0      | 3.81 kt      | 7.33 kt   | 20.1%            |\\n| `mlp-control`                          | 26.3%        | 75.7%     | 16.8\xb0         | 65.6\xb0      | 3.39 kt      | 7.05 kt   | 18.7%            |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled`          | 38.6%        | 122.6%    | 82.9\xb0         | 159.5\xb0     | 4.33 kt      | 7.66 kt   | 34.9%            |\\n\\nG1 threshold: 0.5 \xd7 naive median = 19.2%.\\n\\nClassical non-converged rate at the gate point: 2.0% (non-converged rows count as +\u221e). Classical\\nis far ahead of every model (a median 3.5\xd7 lower than the instantaneous model\'s); G1 asks only\\nwhether the models beat the prior, and closing that gap is G3\'s question.\\n\\n### Intervals\\n\\nPaired bootstrap of the ratio model / naive median range error at zig + 300 s (2,000 resamples of\\nthe 9,784 test scenarios, seed 20261011). A G1 check passes only if the whole 95% interval is below\\n0.5.\\n\\n| Variant       | Model median | Naive median | Classical median | Ratio to naive | 95% interval   | G1 check |\\n| ------------- | ------------ | ------------ | ---------------- | -------------- | -------------- | -------- |\\n| instantaneous | 16.1%        | 38.4%        | 4.6% (0.119)     | 0.419          | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| time-late     | 21.2%        | 38.4%        | 4.6% (0.119)     | 0.551          | [0.538, 0.565] | fail     |\\n\\nThe classical ratio (in brackets) is a point value; no interval was computed for it. The interval\\nreflects which test scenarios were drawn, not how a re-trained model would score (one training\\nseed per model).\\n\\n### Checks\\n\\nMachine verdict: **fail**, because `g1-time-late` and `time-late-not-worse` failed.\\n\\n| Check                         | Result | Detail (median range error; naive and classical beside each)                                            |\\n| ----------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `g1-instantaneous`            | pass   | 16.1% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                          |\\n| `g1-time-late`                | fail   | 21.2% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.551 [0.538, 0.565] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                          |\\n| `time-late-not-worse`         | fail   | time-late worse at 20 of 20 gated curve points (every 60 s to 20 min); 3 later points skipped (n < 500) |\\n| `instantaneous-beats-control` | pass   | zig+300s: 16.1% vs CNN 29.3%; final: 16.4% vs 29.3% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%)        |\\n| `time-late-beats-control`     | pass   | zig+300s: 21.2% vs MLP 26.3%; final: 24.3% vs 28.2% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%)        |\\n| `shuffled-labels-at-naive`    | pass   | 1.005 \xd7 naive (38.6% vs 38.4%; allowed 0.9\u20131.1)                                                         |\\n\\n**Error against time since the zig.** Naive is flat (38\u201349% median throughout). Classical falls\\nfast: 22.6% one minute after the zig, 4.6% at five minutes, 1.6% at fifteen. The instantaneous\\nmodel falls from 24.5% at one minute to 16.1% at five and levels off near 15% at ten to fifteen\\nminutes. The time-late model has the same shape but sits above it everywhere (26.0%, 21.2%,\\n19.0%), although it sees more data at every point. Rounds 1\u20132 read this as the signature of an\\nundertrained model, not of a pipeline that hides the future from it (see the panel\'s checks\\nbelow); round 3 withdrew that explanation for the continued model, which trained to early stopping\\nand is still worse (see the caveat). Interactive curves are on\\nthe [results page](/docs/results/iteration-1).\\n\\n### By range band and zig angle\\n\\nMedian range error at zig + 300 s, with each model\'s ratio to naive in brackets. Bands are by the\\nmean range over the window, so a few test scenarios fall outside 2\u201320 km. Slices with n < 200 are\\nnot interpreted.\\n\\n| Slice          | n     | naive  | classical     | instantaneous | time-late     |\\n| -------------- | ----- | ------ | ------------- | ------------- | ------------- |\\n| range 0\u20132 km   | 132   | 603.7% | 0.7% (0.001)  | 29.9% (0.050) | 53.5% (0.089) |\\n| range 2\u20135 km   | 1,640 | 150.8% | 2.0% (0.013)  | 17.2% (0.114) | 26.2% (0.174) |\\n| range 5\u201310 km  | 3,705 | 23.2%  | 3.8% (0.166)  | 13.6% (0.588) | 18.6% (0.802) |\\n| range 10\u201320 km | 3,741 | 32.5%  | 7.2% (0.222)  | 16.2% (0.498) | 20.0% (0.615) |\\n| range 20\u201330 km | 557   | 58.5%  | 10.4% (0.178) | 28.7% (0.490) | 33.0% (0.564) |\\n| range 30+ km   | 9     | 71.8%  | 8.2% (0.114)  | 27.0% (0.377) | 37.9% (0.529) |\\n| zig 30\u201350\xb0     | 3,232 | 38.7%  | 6.4% (0.166)  | 17.1% (0.441) | 21.6% (0.557) |\\n| zig 50\u201370\xb0     | 3,286 | 37.9%  | 4.3% (0.113)  | 16.0% (0.423) | 21.0% (0.554) |\\n| zig 70\u201390\xb0     | 3,266 | 38.7%  | 3.5% (0.091)  | 15.3% (0.395) | 21.1% (0.545) |\\n\\nThe per-band ratios mostly measure naive, not the models: naive guesses the middle of the prior,\\nso it is very wrong at short range and nearly right at 5\u201310 km. In absolute terms the models show\\nthe prior\'s U-shape (best at 5\u201310 km, worse at both ends), whereas classical\'s error grows steadily\\nwith range, as the geometry predicts (lower bearing rate at long range). Larger zigs help\\nclassical (6.4% \u2192 3.5%) and, weakly, the instantaneous model (17.1% \u2192 15.3%); the time-late model\\nis flat across zig angle (21.0\u201321.6%).\\n\\n### Held-out geometry (20\u201330 km initial range, reported, not gated)\\n\\n10,000 scenarios outside the training range; at zig + 300 s, n = 9,769 (231 excluded).\\n\\n| Estimator            | Range median | Range P90               | Gross-error rate | Ratio to naive |\\n| -------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------- | -------------- |\\n| naive                | 59.7%        | 70.2%                   | 77.6%            | 1              |\\n| classical            | 18.8%        | \u221e (12.6% non-converged) | 23.9%            | 0.315          |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous` | 42.4%        | 57.6%                   | 28.5%            | 0.710          |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`   | 45.9%        | 59.9%                   | 37.1%            | 0.768          |\\n\\nOutside the training range the models lose most of their advantage over naive (ratios 0.71 and\\n0.77, against 0.42 and 0.55 in range), while classical keeps a median of 19% at the cost of more\\nnon-converged solutions. The panel read this as the models being held towards the training prior\\nrather than extrapolating the geometry. G2 is to include a range-extrapolation check.\\n\\n## Attempt 1: why the time-late model was undertrained\\n\\nAll models shared the same budget in **time**, not in epochs: a 5.5 h wall-clock cap per model,\\ninside GitHub\'s 6 h job limit. The bidirectional LSTM runs the window in both\\ndirections and has twice the instantaneous LSTM\'s parameters (82,948 vs 41,476); one epoch took\\nabout 775 s against 276 s. The cap therefore stopped it after 25 epochs while the instantaneous\\nmodel ran all 60. Its validation loss was still falling (0.592, 0.565, 0.571 over the last three\\nepochs; best at epoch 23).\\n\\nThe panel compared like with like. On the post-zig validation rows (the rows that are scored), the\\ntime-late model at epoch 23 had a loss of 0.406; the instantaneous model at the same epoch had\\n0.478 and needed until epoch 31 to reach 0.405. At equal training, time-late was ahead.\\n\\n## What the panel found in attempt 1 (rounds 1\u20132)\\n\\nFive independent agent reviewers (TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics,\\nreproducibility, red team) reviewed attempt 1 over two rounds; the full record, with every finding\\nand response, is in\\n[`experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md).\\n\\nIn plain words:\\n\\n- **The pipeline is sound.** Padding does not leak into the bidirectional pass (padded and\\n  unpadded windows give identical outputs); predictions line up with their scenarios; the causal\\n  models ignore bearings after the time they estimate; the time-late model does use later bearings\\n  (cutting them off makes it worse, 21.2% \u2192 27.2%).\\n- **The instantaneous pass is real.** No truth reaches the inputs or the normalisation, naive is not\\n  artificially weak, every comparison uses the same 9,784 scenarios, and the shuffled-label model\\n  trained down to the \\"know nothing\\" level and scores like naive.\\n- **Reproducible.** A reviewer regenerated the data and re-scored the committed weights locally: the\\n  verdict file came out byte-identical, naive and classical metrics byte-identical, model metrics\\n  within 1.3 \xd7 10\u207b\u2075 relative.\\n- **The time-late fail is a budget effect** (one reviewer rejected in round 1 until this was\\n  recognised). Calling it \\"ML not extracting the signal\\" would overstate it. The first result is\\n  recorded as: _G1 not passed as run; the instantaneous variant met the criterion; the time-late\\n  variant failed under a 25-epoch wall-clock budget and is plausibly undertrained; no evidence that\\n  the time-late signal is absent._\\n- **But no rule allowed a re-run.** Turning \\"undertrained\\" into \\"try again\\" needed an explicit\\n  amendment, decided after seeing the result and written down before any new training. That is\\n  what the user decided (below), and round 2 approved the protocol with all five reviewers at\\n  approve-with-findings.\\n\\nSmaller findings, reported rather than fixed now: pre-zig rows, never scored, carry 64% of the\\ntime-late model\'s training loss; model manifests lack a git commit field (provenance\\nis recorded in the review instead); training budgets should be set in epochs, not wall-clock time,\\nbecause runner CPUs vary; the shuffled model\'s median course error (82.9\xb0) is a little below\\nchance (\u2248 90\xb0), a weak bearing-as-course prior rather than leakage.\\n\\n## The decision and the continuation protocol\\n\\nThe user decided on 12 October, after the first G1 result and before any further training, to\\ncontinue the time-late model **once**, to the same 60-epoch budget the instantaneous model had. SRD\\n\xa75 was amended to allow exactly this, and the protocol was fixed in\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md#user-decision-2026-10-12-after-round-1)\\nbefore the run started:\\n\\n1. **One change only:** no wall-clock cap. Training resumes from the committed epoch-23 weights\\n   (digest checked) and runs to epoch 60 or early stopping (patience 3). Epoch 24 of the first run\\n   is dropped.\\n2. **Unchanged:** data, splits, training seed, architecture, loss, learning rate, batch size. The\\n   other four models are reused untouched; the instantaneous model is not retrained.\\n3. **Optimizer:** Adam\'s state was never saved in attempt 1, so it restarts once, at epoch 24, and\\n   epochs 24\u201326 do not count towards patience. From then on Adam\'s state is saved with every\\n   checkpoint and restored if a job is interrupted, so a resumed run matches an uninterrupted one\\n   (tested: identical weights).\\n4. **Scoring:** the verdict of record comes from re-scoring all five committed models with\\n   `evaluate-g1.yml` on main; the test split is not touched until training completes.\\n5. **Outcomes, fixed in advance:** both intervals wholly below 0.5 and shuffled labels at naive \u2192\\n   **G1 pass**, even if `time-late-not-worse` still fails (the record then says \\"time-late not shown\\n   better than instantaneous\\"). A ratio fail of the continued model is **final**: no further\\n   continuation, seed or architecture change, and the project stops per SRD \xa75.\\n6. **Record:** attempt 1 (0.551) stays recorded as a valid attempt. Every job of the continuation is\\n   listed with its run id, epochs covered and runner CPU.\\n\\n## Attempt 2: the continued time-late model\\n\\nExperiment `20261012-g1-r2`, re-scored by run\\n[38275763335](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38275763335) on main code (commit\\nbb4525b). The four reused models are byte-identical to attempt 1; their metrics differ from\\nattempt 1\'s re-score by at most 8.4 \xd7 10\u207b\u2076 relative (float32 inference on AVX2, then AVX-512), and\\nnaive and classical are identical.\\n\\n**Training.** Run 38208186337, branch `train/i1-continue`, commit 7c1c579. Before training, the\\nseed step checked the epoch-23 weights (digest 2c5f8784\u2026) and a configuration that differed from\\nattempt 1 only in `max_wall_s` (19,800 \u2192 86,400).\\n\\n| Attempt | Job          | Epochs | Runner CPU                | Outcome                                           |\\n| ------- | ------------ | ------ | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\\n| 1       | 114676289268 | 24\u201341  | AMD EPYC 7763             | 345-min step timeout; last checkpoint at epoch 41 |\\n| 1       | 114785366608 | \u2013      | \u2013                         | g1 stopped before scoring; test split untouched   |\\n| 2       | 114789157629 | 42\u201349  | Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C | early stopping, best epoch 46                     |\\n| 2       | 114832540726 | \u2013      | \u2013                         | g1 succeeded; informational folder `20261012-g1/` |\\n\\nThe first job\'s log reads \\"resuming bilstm-time-late after epoch 23 (optimizer state restarts;\\npatience not counted for epochs 24\u201326)\\", the second\'s \\"resuming bilstm-time-late after epoch 41\\n(optimizer state restored)\\". Epochs took about 1,100 s on the AMD runner and 870 s on the Intel one. Epochs 0\u201323 ran in attempt\\n1 (run 38152208243, AMD EPYC 9V74), so training crossed three CPU families and a retrain will not\\nreproduce the weights byte for byte. The committed weights (c52b628c\u2026) are the artefact of record;\\nre-scoring them is deterministic, and a reviewer reproduced this experiment folder byte for byte.\\nExtracted log lines are in\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1/training-logs/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261012-g1/training-logs).\\n\\nTraining stopped after 50 epochs (best 46), stop reason `early_stopping`, against the\\ninstantaneous model\'s 60. Validation loss at the best epoch was 0.441 (attempt 1\'s best: 0.565 at\\nepoch 23).\\n\\n**The epoch-48 spike.** Train and validation loss jumped at epoch 48 (validation 0.447 \u2192 0.535,\\nthen 0.448 at epoch 49). It is a training instability, not a resume artefact: the instantaneous\\nmodel, never resumed, had one at epoch 40. The scored weights are from epoch 46, before it. The\\nspike contributed to the patience stop at epoch 49, so the \\"equal epoch budget\\" was in effect 50\\nagainst 60; whether epoch 48 would otherwise have improved is unknown. Gradient clipping is an\\niteration-2 candidate.\\n\\n### At the gate point (zig + 300 s, test split)\\n\\nn = 9,784 scenarios (216 excluded), the same for every estimator, as in attempt 1.\\n\\n| Estimator                      | Range median | Range P90 | Course median | Course P90 | Speed median | Speed P90 | Gross-error rate |\\n| ------------------------------ | ------------ | --------- | ------------- | ---------- | ------------ | --------- | ---------------- |\\n| naive                          | 38.4%        | 161.5%    | 90.0\xb0         | 163.0\xb0     | 4.30 kt      | 7.67 kt   | 35.7%            |\\n| classical                      | 4.6%         | 29.2%     | 1.9\xb0          | 23.7\xb0      | 0.64 kt      | 5.19 kt   | 5.7%             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`           | 16.1%        | 39.6%     | 9.4\xb0          | 41.7\xb0      | 2.16 kt      | 5.59 kt   | 5.4%             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late` (continued) | 17.3%        | 43.5%     | 9.6\xb0          | 41.1\xb0      | 2.39 kt      | 5.71 kt   | 7.3%             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`           | 29.3%        | 69.7%     | 23.0\xb0         | 78.4\xb0      | 3.81 kt      | 7.33 kt   | 20.1%            |\\n| `mlp-control`                  | 26.3%        | 75.7%     | 16.8\xb0         | 65.6\xb0      | 3.39 kt      | 7.05 kt   | 18.7%            |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled`  | 38.6%        | 122.6%    | 82.9\xb0         | 159.5\xb0     | 4.33 kt      | 7.66 kt   | 34.9%            |\\n\\nG1 threshold: 0.5 \xd7 naive median = 19.2%. Classical non-converged rate at the gate point: 2.0%.\\nClassical is still far ahead: its median is 3.5\xd7 lower than the instantaneous model\'s and 3.8\xd7\\nlower than the time-late model\'s.\\n\\n### Intervals, both attempts\\n\\nPaired bootstrap of model / naive median range error at zig + 300 s (2,000 resamples of the 9,784\\ntest scenarios, seed 20261011). A G1 check passes only if the whole 95% interval is below 0.5.\\nMedians are of range error; classical\'s ratio to naive is 0.119, a point value (no interval\\ncomputed).\\n\\n| Attempt, variant | Model | Naive | Classical | Ratio | 95% interval   | G1 check |\\n| ---------------- | ----- | ----- | --------- | ----- | -------------- | -------- |\\n| 1, instantaneous | 16.1% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.419 | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| 1, time-late     | 21.2% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.551 | [0.538, 0.565] | fail     |\\n| 2, instantaneous | 16.1% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.419 | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| 2, time-late     | 17.3% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.450 | [0.438, 0.464] | pass     |\\n\\nThe time-late upper bound is 0.036 below 0.5, about 5 bootstrap standard errors; its point\\nestimate is about 7 below. The statistics reviewer\'s independent bootstrap agreed (time-late\\n[0.4377, 0.4649], instantaneous [0.4074, 0.4313]). The intervals reflect which test scenarios were\\ndrawn, not how a re-trained model would score: each variant was trained once.\\n\\n### Checks\\n\\nMachine verdict: **inconclusive**, from `time-late-not-worse` (tiered rule 2). `verdict.json` is\\nleft exactly as the code wrote it; the pass is the panel\'s application of rule 1.\\n\\n| Check                         | Result | Detail (median range error; naive and classical beside each)                                     |\\n| ----------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\\n| `g1-instantaneous`            | pass   | 16.1% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                   |\\n| `g1-time-late`                | pass   | 17.3% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.450 [0.438, 0.464] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                   |\\n| `time-late-not-worse`         | fail   | more than 5% worse than instantaneous at 14 of 20 gated points; 3 later points skipped (n < 500) |\\n| `instantaneous-beats-control` | pass   | zig+300s: 16.1% vs CNN 29.3%; final: 16.4% vs 29.3% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%) |\\n| `time-late-beats-control`     | pass   | zig+300s: 17.3% vs MLP 26.3%; final: 18.5% vs 28.2% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%) |\\n| `shuffled-labels-at-naive`    | pass   | 1.005 \xd7 naive (38.6% vs 38.4%; allowed 0.9\u20131.1)                                                  |\\n\\n**Error against time since the zig** (median range error, test split):\\n\\n| Time since zig | n      | naive | classical | instantaneous | time-late (continued) | time-late / instantaneous |\\n| -------------- | ------ | ----- | --------- | ------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------- |\\n| 60 s           | 10,000 | 40.3% | 22.6%     | 24.5%         | 24.1%                 | 0.984                     |\\n| 300 s          | 9,784  | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 16.1%         | 17.3%                 | 1.075                     |\\n| 600 s          | 6,512  | 39.1% | 2.3%      | 14.9%         | 15.7%                 | 1.054                     |\\n| 900 s          | 3,116  | 43.4% | 1.6%      | 14.5%         | 15.5%                 | 1.069                     |\\n| 1200 s         | 516    | 48.9% | 1.4%      | 17.0%         | 16.5%                 | 0.973                     |\\n\\nThe continuation closed most of the gap to the instantaneous model (attempt 1\'s time-late model:\\n26.0%, 21.2% and 19.0% at one, five and ten minutes) but did not cross it.\\n\\n### By range band and zig angle\\n\\nMedian range error at zig + 300 s, ratio to naive in brackets. Slices with n < 200 are not\\ninterpreted.\\n\\n| Slice          | n     | naive  | classical     | instantaneous | time-late (continued) |\\n| -------------- | ----- | ------ | ------------- | ------------- | --------------------- |\\n| range 0\u20132 km   | 132   | 603.7% | 0.7% (0.001)  | 29.9% (0.050) | 46.2% (0.077)         |\\n| range 2\u20135 km   | 1,640 | 150.8% | 2.0% (0.013)  | 17.2% (0.114) | 21.2% (0.141)         |\\n| range 5\u201310 km  | 3,705 | 23.2%  | 3.8% (0.166)  | 13.6% (0.588) | 15.8% (0.683)         |\\n| range 10\u201320 km | 3,741 | 32.5%  | 7.2% (0.222)  | 16.2% (0.498) | 15.6% (0.480)         |\\n| range 20\u201330 km | 557   | 58.5%  | 10.4% (0.178) | 28.7% (0.490) | 27.5% (0.471)         |\\n| range 30+ km   | 9     | 71.8%  | 8.2% (0.114)  | 27.0% (0.377) | 30.3% (0.422)         |\\n| zig 30\u201350\xb0     | 3,232 | 38.7%  | 6.4% (0.166)  | 17.1% (0.441) | 18.3% (0.473)         |\\n| zig 50\u201370\xb0     | 3,286 | 37.9%  | 4.3% (0.113)  | 16.0% (0.423) | 17.0% (0.449)         |\\n| zig 70\u201390\xb0     | 3,266 | 38.7%  | 3.5% (0.091)  | 15.3% (0.395) | 16.6% (0.429)         |\\n\\nThe time-late model\'s deficit is at 2\u201310 km; at 10\u201330 km it is level with, or slightly ahead of,\\nthe instantaneous model. Both models still follow the training prior\'s U-shape across range bands,\\nwhereas classical\'s error grows steadily with range.\\n\\n### Held-out geometry (20\u201330 km initial range, reported, not gated)\\n\\nn = 9,769 at zig + 300 s (231 excluded).\\n\\n| Estimator                      | Range median | Range P90               | Gross-error rate | Ratio to naive |\\n| ------------------------------ | ------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------- | -------------- |\\n| naive                          | 59.7%        | 70.2%                   | 77.6%            | 1              |\\n| classical                      | 18.8%        | \u221e (12.6% non-converged) | 23.9%            | 0.315          |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`           | 42.4%        | 57.6%                   | 28.5%            | 0.710          |\\n| `bilstm-time-late` (continued) | 43.9%        | 58.5%                   | 31.1%            | 0.735          |\\n\\nAs in attempt 1, both models are held towards the training prior outside the training range.\\n\\n## What the panel decided (round 3)\\n\\nThe same five specialist reviewers judged the continuation. The full record is in\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1-r2/review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261012-g1-r2/review.md).\\n\\n| Reviewer                | Attempt 1 (rounds 1\u20132)                   | Round 3               | Round 3 re-invitation |\\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------------------- | --------------------- |\\n| TMA geometry            | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n| Estimation and numerics | reject \u2192 approve-with-findings (round 2) | approve-with-findings | approve               |\\n| Evaluation statistics   | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n| Reproducibility         | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve               |\\n| Red team                | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n\\nAll five said the evidence supports **G1 pass under pre-registered rule 1**. The reproducibility\\nreviewer re-scored the committed weights locally and got `verdict.json`, `bootstrap.json`,\\nmetrics, curves and held-out results byte-identical; the red team found no protocol deviation,\\nleakage or selection between attempts (one continuation, one re-score).\\n\\n**One major finding (M1, red team; geometry agreed).** The SRD \xa75 G1 row still said a time-late\\nconsistency failure makes G1 inconclusive, and did not carry rule 1; as the SRD wins conflicts,\\nthe pass needed it there. The row was amended to carry rule 1 as the user decided it on\\n12 October before the run. Rule 1 was committed at 05:29:44Z and published in PR #28 at\\n05:30:16Z, 17 s after the run was triggered (05:29:59Z) but before any continuation epoch finished\\n(about 05:49Z) and about 8 h before the test split was scored (13:25Z). The round-1 explanation\\nof the consistency failure (undertraining) is withdrawn for the continued model.\\n\\nMinor findings, answered in the record: the caveat now states the gap rather than only \\"not shown\\nbetter\\"; the epoch-48 spike (above); a cross-process resume test for the bidirectional layer,\\nwhich a reviewer ran (byte-identical weights) and which iteration 2 adds to the suite; the per-job\\nrecord and both CPUs, now in the review; re-scored manifests lack `cpu_model`, which\\n`evaluate-g1.yml` will record in iteration 2. The re-invitation corrected the wording of the gap,\\nthe margin and the spike. No reviewer rejects and no blocker or major finding is open; the\\nverdict\'s `decided_by` names round 3.\\n\\n## Outcome: G1 passes, with a caveat\\n\\n**G1 passes** under pre-registered rule 1 (SRD \xa75 as amended 2026-10-12). Both ratio intervals\\nlie wholly below 0.5, instantaneous 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] and time-late (continued to 50 epochs)\\n0.450 [0.438, 0.464], and the shuffled-label model sits at naive. A small network does extract\\nrange information from bearings alone, in real time and in reconstruction, though at the gate\\npoint it is still about 3.5\xd7 (instantaneous) and 3.8\xd7 (time-late) worse than the classical solver.\\n\\n**Caveat, recorded with the pass:**\\n\\n- **Time-late not shown better than instantaneous.** It is measurably worse: at zig+300s,\\n  0.173 vs 0.161, a ratio of 1.075 [1.046, 1.108]. Its median is higher at 18 of the 20 gated\\n  points, by more than 5% (up to 9%) at 14 of them (120\u2013480, 600 and 720\u20131020 s). It is lower\\n  only at zig+60s (0.984 [0.964, 1.006]) and zig+1200s (0.973 [0.864, 1.126], n 516); neither\\n  difference is significant.\\n- **Not explained by undertraining.** The continued model trained to early stopping.\\n- **The BiLSTM under-uses the future.** Its error profile is shaped like a causal estimator\'s.\\n  Masking and the backward pass are verified correct, and truncating the future does change\\n  outputs, but weakly.\\n- **One training draw per variant.** Training-seed variance is unquantified.\\n\\n## What G1 cannot tell us\\n\\n- The targets move at constant velocity and the ownship zig is instantaneous; nothing here says how\\n  the models cope with manoeuvres or gradual turns.\\n- \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0 and no dropouts only; noise and gaps are iteration 2\'s question (G2).\\n- The time-late model\'s estimate at zig + 300 s uses bearings recorded after that time. It is a\\n  post-hoc reconstruction, not a real-time solution.\\n- The interval covers test sampling, not training variation: each model was trained once, and\\n  weights differ across runner CPU families.\\n- The time-late model was trained in three stretches on three CPU families with one optimizer\\n  restart; the instantaneous model in one. The protocol equalised the epoch budget, not the\\n  training path, and early stopping ended the time-late model at 50 epochs against 60.\\n- The shuffled-label test rules out leakage through labels and the training harness; truth hidden\\n  in the inputs is ruled out by a separate counterfactual test.\\n\\nThe full list, with mitigations, is in\\n[`specs/002-iteration-1-signal/g1-limits.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/specs/002-iteration-1-signal/g1-limits.md).\\n\\n## Next: iteration 2\\n\\nIteration 2 (spec 003) runs the same models on \u03c3 = 1\u20132\xb0 with 10% dropouts, judged at G2: median\\nrange error below 20% and course below 10\xb0 five minutes after the zig, speed below 1 kt ten\\nminutes after, at \u03c3 = 1\xb0. At \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0 the instantaneous model is at 16.1% and 9.4\xb0 (zig + 300 s)\\nand 1.83 kt (zig + 600 s), against classical\'s 4.6%, 1.9\xb0 and 0.31 kt, so speed is furthest off.\\nLeads from this gate:\\n\\n- Pre-zig rows carry 64% of the time-late model\'s training loss but are never scored.\\n- Gradient clipping, against spikes like the one at epoch 48.\\n- Both models still follow the training prior by range band, and are held to the training prior\\n  on the held-out 20\u201330 km set; G2 is to include a range-extrapolation check.\\n- Why the bidirectional model under-uses the future, before time-late is relied on.\\n- Engineering: training budgets in epochs rather than wall-clock time; the runner CPU recorded in\\n  re-scored manifests; a cross-process bidirectional resume test; a git commit in model manifests."},{"id":"/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-11-g0-pipeline-verified.md","title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","description":"The first gate asks a narrow question: can we trust the data before training anything on it?","date":"2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":1.12,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","date":"2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/46/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1"}},"content":"The first gate asks a narrow question: can we trust the data before training anything on it?\\n\\n**Verdict: G0 pass** (machine verdict; team review pending). Experiment:\\n`experiments/20261011-g0/`.\\n\\n- 100,000 noise-free scenarios generated (seed 42); the 10,000-scenario test split was scored.\\n- The least-squares solver recovered truth on **10,000 / 10,000** scenarios. Worst cases: 2.9\xd710\u207b\u2074\\n  relative range, 0.016\xb0 course, 0.002 kt speed (tolerances 10\u207b\xb3, 0.1\xb0, 0.01 kt). Median range\\n  error 1.9\xd710\u207b\u2077.\\n- The naive baseline reproduced exactly across two independent fits.\\n\\n## Yardsticks for iteration 1\\n\\nOn the same noise-free test set, the naive baseline\'s median range error is 40% (P90 139%), its\\nmedian course error 90\xb0 and its gross-error rate 37%. Any model must beat these to pass G1.\\n\\nOn noisy data (\u03c3 0.5\u20132\xb0, up to 10% dropouts) the classical solver\'s median range error is about 5%,\\nwith P90 \u2248 43% and gross-error rate \u2248 9%. None of those gross errors were optimiser failures:\\nstarting from the true state never found a lower cost. The tail comes from the information in the\\nbearings, not from a weak solver.\\n\\n## Caveat\\n\\nPassing G0 shows that generator and solver agree. On its own it would not catch an error shared by\\nboth; hand-computed geometry tests cover that separately.\\n\\nGeneration plus the G0 run took about 70 seconds on a laptop-class machine."}]}}')}}]);